  "name": "limit-string-length-over-limit",
  "description": "A ref string of 1,025 Unicode code points, one past the maximum, measured before NFC normalisation, is refused before any further validation runs.",
  "identity_version": "spec1",
  "classification": "negative",
  "coverage": [
    "limits.string-length.over-limit"
  ],
  "notes": "The operative ref is \"test/\" (5 code points) followed by 510 repetitions of \"e\" U+0065 plus COMBINING ACUTE ACCENT U+0301, pre-NFC decomposed spelling: 1,025 code points before normalisation, one past the maximum. NFC composes each decomposed pair into a single precomposed \"é\" U+00E9, so the same ref is only 515 code points after normalisation, well under the limit. String length is counted after JSON escape decoding but before NFC normalisation, so a conformant implementation refuses this input at its true pre-NFC length; an implementation that normalises first and counts after NFC would wrongly accept it, and is caught. input_bytes is required for two independent reasons here: this is a structural-limit vector, so it must begin at the strict parse stage; and it is byte-sensitive in the strongest sense, since a JSON round-trip through any tool's own writer could NFC-launder the decomposed bytes into precomposed form and silently destroy the case under test.",
  "input_bytes": "",
  "failure_category": "input_limit_exceeded"
}
